%N A183632 T(n,k) = number of (n+1) X (k+1) 0..2 arrays with every 2 X 2 subblock summing to 4.
%C A183632 Proof of the formula for T(n,k): Let a(i,j), 0 <= i <= n, 0 <= j <= k, be the entries of the array, and put u(i,j) = a(i,j) if i+j is even and u(i,j) = 2 - a(i,j) if i+j is odd; this maps the 0..2 arrays bijectively to the 0..2 arrays. A 2 X 2 block of a sums to 4 if and only if u(i,j) + u(i+1,j+1) = u(i,j+1) + u(i+1,j). This holds for all blocks if and only if u(i+1,j) - u(i,j) does not depend on j, that is, if and only if u(i,j) = p(i) + q(j) with integers p(0..n) and q(0..k); here p and q are unique if we require min p = 0. Let r = max p. Then 0 <= u(i,j) <= 2 for all i, j if and only if r <= 2 and 0 <= q(j) <= 2-r for all j. For r = 0, 1, 2 there are 1, 2^(n+1)-2, 3^(n+1)-2^(n+2)+1 vectors p (n+1 entries in 0..r, with minimum 0 and maximum r) and 3^(k+1), 2^(k+1), 1 vectors q, so T(n,k) = 3^(k+1) + (2^(n+1)-2)*2^(k+1) + 3^(n+1) - 2^(n+2) + 1, which is the formula in the Formula section. - _Daniel Veljjanoski_, Nov 18 2026
%H A183632 R. H. Hardin, <a href="/A183632/b183632.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..219</a>
%F A183632 Empirical, for every row and column: a(n) = 6*a(n-1)-11*a(n-2)+6*a(n-3).
%F A183632 The coefficient of a(n-i) is -s(4,4-i), s() being the Stirling number of the first kind, via _D. S. McNeil_ and _M. F. Hasler_ in the Sequence Fans Mailing List.
%F A183632 For a 0..z array with 2X2 blocks summing to 2z, the coefficients are -s(z+2,z+2-i).
%F A183632 T(n,k) = 3^(n+1) + 3^(k+1) + 2^(n+k+2) - 2^(n+2) - 2^(k+2) + 1 (see the Comments section for a proof). For fixed k this is a linear combination of 1, 2^n and 3^n, which proves the empirical recurrence above for every row and column, and also the formulas conjectured for the columns in A183624-A183626 and A183629-A183631. - _Daniel Veljjanoski_, Nov 18 2026
%F A183632 For (n+1) X (k+1) 0..z arrays with every 2 X 2 block summing to 2z the same proof gives the count (z+1)^(k+1) + Sum_{r=1..z} ((r+1)^(n+1) - 2*r^(n+1) + (r-1)^(n+1))*(z+1-r)^(k+1). For fixed k (and, by transposing the array, for fixed n) this is a linear combination of 1, 2^n, ..., (z+1)^n, which proves the statement above on the coefficients -s(z+2,z+2-i). - _Daniel Veljjanoski_, Nov 18 2026
